# Cursor operating protocol

**LIVE_MODULAR_PUBLISHING_ENABLED.** Read `.dentcast/workflows/global/README.md` and all complete active global sections selected by that router for the current edit scope, plus the complete `.dentcast/workflows/agent-parity.md`. They govern conversation, approvals, capability failures, Git, builders and final verification. Legacy files are backup only; do not read them for execution.
New publication follows `.dentcast/workflows/README.md` and the applicable full modules. Staging receipts never replace scientific execution or founder consent.

## Interrupted publication

For «انتشار ناقص داشتم» or an explicit request to resume a publication the user identifies as incomplete/interrupted, read `.dentcast/PUBLISH-HANDOFF.md` exactly once, then `.dentcast/workflows/publish-handoff.md`. A generic «ادامه بده» or «ادامه انتشار» alone does not trigger recovery. During the active run, keep state in the current session and do not reread the handoff before each step; use the latest helper digest. Before a new publication, check only whether the file exists. If it does, make no changes and ask whether the user means to resume; read it only after explicit confirmation. Never overwrite unfinished work. Update the handoff after every canonical step and before handing off; delete it only after verified completion. A missing file or unavailable work must be reported, not invented. Local commits and dirty files are not automatically available in another checkout.

## Mandatory per-step checkpoints

Read `.dentcast/workflows/publish-checkpoints.md`. Use the full canonical ledger from `tools/dentcast_publish_handoff.py plan`; write `started` before each action and its actual result afterward using `step`/`record`. Prefer `advance` to record a verified result and start the next ready step in one call, or record explicit consecutive skips; it preserves a separate durable write for every transition. Phase F uses the real `verify` command. Completion is `finish`, authorized cleanup commit/push, then `confirm-cleanup`; restore the same fixed handoff with `restore-cleanup` if cleanup fails. Hooks are supplemental and never mark scientific or semantic work complete.
